<section aria-labelledby="answer"><h2 id="answer">Your old X11 applications have not vanished</h2>
<p>The phrase “KDE removes X11” can sound as though an entire shelf of familiar software has just become museum material. I like old software, but that is not what this release does. The important distinction is between the desktop session you log into and the protocol an individual application uses.</p>
<p><strong>Plasma 6.8 retires the Plasma X11 login session. Legacy X11 applications can generally continue through XWayland inside a Wayland session.</strong> KDE’s <a href="https://kde.org/announcements/plasma/6/6.8.0/">14 October 2026 release announcement</a> also says KDE applications retain X11 support. Other desktops and previously released Plasma versions are separate projects and releases.</p>
<p>This guide is a decision aid, with source and package checks dated <time datetime="2026-11-05">5 November 2026</time>. It contains no new benchmarks from Cthulhu, my Arch Linux desktop with AMD Radeon graphics, and no claim that Plasma 6.8 is installed there. A successful migration is measured by your actual work, including the obscure shortcut you use fifty times a day.</p></section>

<section aria-labelledby="architecture"><h2 id="architecture">X11, Wayland, KWin and the login screen</h2>
<p>X11 is the long-established display protocol behind traditional Linux graphical sessions. An X server connects applications to input and displays; a window manager arranges windows, while compositing adds the final visual composition. Modern rendering has evolved considerably around that architecture. Its age alone is not an explanation of either its strengths or its limitations.</p>
<p>Wayland defines communication between applications and a compositor. In this architecture the compositor is also the display server. In Plasma, KWin handles that role, integrating window management, input routing and presentation. An application provides content for its surfaces; the compositor decides how those surfaces appear on the available outputs. The <a href="https://wayland.freedesktop.org/architecture.html">Wayland architecture documentation</a> explains the relationship.</p>
<p>The display manager is the login interface that starts a chosen session. It is not the same component as KWin. Losing “Plasma (X11)” from that chooser does not mean the login manager itself cannot start another desktop’s X11 session. Nor does a working login screen prove that every application backend has been tested.</p>
<h3>Two application paths, one desktop</h3>
<p>The original diagram shows two paths. A native Wayland application communicates with KWin directly using Wayland. An X11 application communicates with XWayland, which acts as a Wayland client of KWin. KWin then presents both alongside one another through the graphics stack and displays.</p>
<p><a href="https://wayland.freedesktop.org/docs/book/Xwayland.html">XWayland is an X server</a>, not a simplistic translation of every X11 capability into a global Wayland equivalent. It preserves many ordinary application behaviors. Desktop components designed to control the whole X server, including replacement X11 window managers, do not automatically become managers of native Wayland windows.</p>
<p>It also does not automatically isolate X11 applications from one another when they share one XWayland server. Native Wayland’s boundaries and compatibility support can coexist without making every legacy process a fully sandboxed process. This matters when discussing security advantages honestly.</p></section>

<section aria-labelledby="change"><h2 id="change">What changed in 6.8, and why</h2>
<p>Developer David Edmundson’s <a href="https://planet.kde.org/david-edmundson-2026-06-02-ex-11-prepping-for-plasmas-last-x11-supported-release/">2 June 2026 transition explanation</a> identifies Plasma 6.7 as the last release with the Plasma X11 session. It distinguishes removal of session-specific code from continued XWayland support and KDE application compatibility on other desktops. The October release announcement confirms the shipped transition.</p>
<p>Maintaining two desktop paths consumes engineering and testing effort. Focusing KWin and Plasma development on one architecture can simplify future work. That is a project-maintenance argument, not proof that every specialized workflow is already equivalent. The developer explanation acknowledges that scripts, tools and habits may need changes.</p>
<p>Wayland’s compositor-centered approach provides a useful basis for per-output scaling, different refresh rates and modern presentation features. HDR and color management still depend on the compositor, application, driver and display path. A protocol name alone does not establish correct color or reduced latency for a particular machine.</p>
<p>Security changes also affect behavior. An ordinary application should not freely read every other window or inject arbitrary input across the desktop. Some X11 utilities relied on precisely those capabilities. Replacing the capability with an explicit desktop service can preserve the user’s task while changing the tool that performs it.</p></section>

<section aria-labelledby="session"><h2 id="session">Check the session you are actually using</h2>
<p>Run these read-only checks in a terminal inside your graphical session. An SSH shell, a stale tmux environment or a manually modified environment can describe something else. Start with the broad session type:</p>
<pre data-copy="graphical session type" tabindex="0"><code>echo "$XDG_SESSION_TYPE"</code></pre>
<p>A typical value is <code>wayland</code> or <code>x11</code>. Empty output is inconclusive. Next inspect the display connection variables:</p>
<pre data-copy="Wayland display connection" tabindex="0"><code>echo "$WAYLAND_DISPLAY"</code></pre>
<pre data-copy="X11 or XWayland display connection" tabindex="0"><code>echo "$DISPLAY"</code></pre>
<p>A populated <code>DISPLAY</code> in a Wayland session is normal when XWayland is available. It does not turn the whole desktop into an X11 session. Likewise, a session capable of running native Wayland clients can also run applications that choose an X11 backend.</p>
<p>On a system using systemd-logind, and when <code>XDG_SESSION_ID</code> is set for the relevant session, this supplies another view:</p>
<pre data-copy="logind session type when the session ID is set" tabindex="0"><code>loginctl show-session "$XDG_SESSION_ID" -p Type</code></pre>
<p>If that variable is empty, identify the appropriate session with <code>loginctl list-sessions</code> rather than substituting a random ID. These are diagnostic observations, not compatibility tests. An application’s own diagnostics may be needed to establish its rendering backend.</p></section>

<section aria-labelledby="compatibility"><h2 id="compatibility">Compatibility is a workflow question</h2>
<p>This matrix describes architectural expectations and checks, not a certification of every application release. “Typically works” still needs testing with your packages. “Depends” means the application, compositor, portal or driver must implement the relevant path.</p>
<div class="table-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Wayland workflow compatibility"><table><caption>Practical compatibility checks for a Plasma Wayland session</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Workflow</th><th scope="col">Expectation</th><th scope="col">Concrete test</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><th scope="row">Browser, office, terminal</th><td>Typically works with a supported backend</td><td>Text input, file dialogs, printing, scaling, drag and drop</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">X11-only application</th><td>Typically runs through XWayland; verify individually</td><td>Clipboard, focus, dialogs and fractional scaling</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Steam / Proton / Wine</th><td>Depends on game, runtime, backend and driver</td><td>Launch, fullscreen, mouse capture, suspend/resume</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Overlays and game capture</th><td>Verify case by case</td><td>Visible overlay, correct target capture, frame pacing</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Graphics and creative tools</th><td>Verify case by case</td><td>Tablet pressure, shortcuts, color, HDR and exports</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Screenshots</th><td>Desktop-native tool or portal path</td><td>Window selection and correct output scale</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Recording and screen sharing</th><td>Depends on application and portal integration</td><td>Window / monitor source, audio, cursor and consent dialog</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Remote desktop</th><td>Depends on server, client and session mode</td><td>Locked screen, reconnect, clipboard, headless use</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Global hotkeys</th><td>May need reconfiguration or portal support</td><td>Activation while a different app has focus</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Clipboard and auto-type</th><td>Ordinary copy/paste typically works; automation needs review</td><td>Native ↔ XWayland, password-manager workflow</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Window scripts / old X utilities</th><td>X11 assumptions often need replacement</td><td>Move and identify native windows; inspect supported KWin APIs</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Assistive technology</th><td>Verify the exact tool and access needs</td><td>Screen reader, magnification, input assistance and recovery</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>A failure to capture native windows from an X11-only utility can be an architectural limitation, not a Plasma 6.8 regression. Conversely, a crash in a particular package deserves its own bug report. Avoid merging those two categories into “Wayland is broken” or “your workflow is invalid.”</p></section>

<section aria-labelledby="portals"><h2 id="portals">Portals and PipeWire: permission instead of unrestricted capture</h2>
<p>Desktop portals provide standard interfaces for tasks mediated by the desktop. An application can request screen sharing; the desktop backend presents the selection or permission interaction. In Plasma, the KDE portal backend is part of the integration to check. An installed portal package and an application actually using the interface are separate requirements.</p>
<p>The <a href="https://flatpak.github.io/xdg-desktop-portal/docs/doc-org.freedesktop.portal.ScreenCast.html">ScreenCast portal</a> returns selected capture streams through PipeWire. PipeWire transports the media; it does not independently decide that an arbitrary application may record everything. Screen capture and audio output also use related infrastructure without being the same diagnostic problem. For sound problems, the <a href="/blog/linux-audio-troubleshooting/">Linux Audio Rescue guide</a> examines that other path.</p>
<p>There are also portal interfaces for <a href="https://flatpak.github.io/xdg-desktop-portal/docs/doc-org.freedesktop.portal.GlobalShortcuts.html">global shortcuts</a> and <a href="https://flatpak.github.io/xdg-desktop-portal/docs/doc-org.freedesktop.portal.RemoteDesktop.html">remote desktop</a>. Their existence is not proof of universal implementation. Check both ends: the application’s support and the desktop backend supplied by the distribution.</p>
<p>Remote assistance deserves extra care. Sharing an already logged-in desktop, reaching a locked machine and starting an unattended headless session are different capabilities. Test the exact one you need before relying on it remotely. A working local screen-share dialog does not prove that reconnecting after a reboot will work.</p></section>

<section aria-labelledby="readiness"><h2 id="readiness">Choose your Wayland readiness checks</h2>
<p>This private guide narrows the checklist to a use case. It never returns an automatic “compatible” result. Without JavaScript, expand any of the sections below. With JavaScript, selecting a role opens its checks; “all” restores the complete set.</p>
<div class="check-guide" data-reader-guide="wayland"><h3>Test what you rely on</h3><div data-selector hidden><label for="wayland-use">Your main workflow</label><select id="wayland-use"><option value="all">Show all workflows</option><option value="office">Everyday office</option><option value="gaming">Gaming</option><option value="development">Development and automation</option><option value="remote">Remote desktop</option><option value="creative">Creative work and accessibility</option></select></div>
<details class="reader-path" data-guide-panel="office"><summary>Everyday office</summary><p>Test browser calls with both a window and a monitor shared. Check microphone, camera, clipboard, printing and the display scale you actually use. Include docking and suspend/resume. A successful terminal launch is only the start of this test.</p></details>
<details class="reader-path" data-guide-panel="gaming"><summary>Gaming</summary><p>Use your existing supported Steam/Proton/Wine stack. Test a must-have game with its usual overlays and capture tool. Check mouse confinement, alt-tab, multiple displays, VRR and frame pacing. Record versions when comparing results; this page supplies no universal FPS prediction.</p></details>
<details class="reader-path" data-guide-panel="development"><summary>Development and automation</summary><p>Inventory scripts that call xdotool, wmctrl, xrandr or inspect X window IDs. Identify the desired task, then find a supported KWin or application interface. Test IDE shortcuts and screen sharing. Do not globally force every Qt or GTK application to a backend merely to fix one program.</p></details>
<details class="reader-path" data-guide-panel="remote"><summary>Remote desktop</summary><p>Test the real server/client pair from another device. Include locked-screen behavior, permission prompts, clipboard and reconnection. Check unattended and headless requirements separately. Keep another administration route until those tests succeed; do not expose a remote desktop directly just for this experiment.</p></details>
<details class="reader-path" data-guide-panel="creative"><summary>Creative work and accessibility</summary><p>Open a real project. Test tablet pressure, calibration, color-management settings, HDR where needed and display scaling. Include assistive input, magnification and screen-reader navigation if you use them. A blocker in essential accessibility is a legitimate reason to delay a migration on a supported system.</p></details>
</div></section>

<section aria-labelledby="distributions"><h2 id="distributions">Debian, Arch, Ubuntu and Fedora do not update together</h2>
<p>An upstream release date does not change every installed desktop overnight. The following package-page snapshot was checked on 5 November. It describes the referenced repositories, not every backport, architecture or locally installed machine. Package revision prefixes such as Debian’s epoch are packaging information, not part of the Plasma marketing version.</p>
<div class="table-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Plasma distribution package snapshot"><table><caption>plasma-desktop package references · 5 November 2026</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Repository / release</th><th scope="col">Published package</th><th scope="col">Implication</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><th scope="row"><a href="https://packages.debian.org/trixie/plasma-desktop">Debian 13 Trixie</a></th><td><code>4:6.3.6-1</code></td><td>Upstream 6.8 change does not retroactively remove its session.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row"><a href="https://archlinux.org/packages/extra/x86_64/plasma-desktop/">Arch Extra, x86_64</a></th><td><code>6.8.2-1</code></td><td>Repository is already on the 6.8 line; inspect installed state.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row"><a href="https://packages.ubuntu.com/plasma-desktop">Ubuntu 26.10 / Stonking</a></th><td><code>4:6.7.5-0ubuntu1</code></td><td>The referenced standard package is still 6.7.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row"><a href="https://packages.ubuntu.com/plasma-desktop">Ubuntu 26.04 LTS / Resolute</a></th><td><code>4:6.6.4-0ubuntu1</code>; updates <code>4:6.6.6-0ubuntu0.1</code></td><td>Use the correct release and pocket.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row"><a href="https://packages.fedoraproject.org/pkgs/plasma-desktop/plasma-desktop/">Fedora package overview</a></th><td>44: <code>6.8.2-1.fc44</code>; 45: <code>6.8.2-1.fc45</code>; 43: <code>6.7.5-1.fc43</code></td><td>Different Fedora rows also differ; check your release and update channel.</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>On Debian/Ubuntu, <code>apt-cache policy plasma-desktop</code> distinguishes installed and candidate versions. Arch users can inspect <code>pacman -Q plasma-desktop</code>; Fedora users can inspect <code>rpm -q plasma-desktop</code>. These read-only queries do not establish the whole KWin, driver and portal stack. Follow the distribution’s normal supported upgrade process and avoid partial package mixes.</p></section>

<section aria-labelledby="graphics"><h2 id="graphics">AMD, NVIDIA, mixed monitors and gaming</h2>
<p>AMD systems commonly rely on a kernel driver, Mesa and the compositor working together. NVIDIA proprietary-driver setups add their own integration requirements. “AMD works” and “NVIDIA fails” are both too broad to guide an actual installation. GPU generation, driver branch, XWayland, KWin, connectors and application backend all matter.</p>
<p>KDE’s <a href="https://community.kde.org/Plasma/Wayland/Nvidia">NVIDIA guidance</a> discusses EGL integration, DRM modesetting and explicit synchronization. Treat older minimum-version statements as historical requirements, not recommendations to install an obsolete driver. Use the maintained driver supported by your distribution and hardware. This guide does not prescribe a global kernel-parameter rewrite.</p>
<p>For a concrete version-scoped example, NVIDIA’s <a href="https://download.nvidia.com/XFree86/Linux-x86_64/580.178.04/README/wayland-issues.html">580.178.04 Wayland known-issues chapter</a> documents limits in nvidia-settings and particular GLX paths. That is documentation for that driver branch, not a list of defects newly introduced by Plasma 6.8. Read the release notes for your installed driver before turning an old workaround into a new system-wide setting.</p>
<p>For multiple displays, test the intended combination of scale, refresh rate and orientation. Then repeat after suspend, unplugging a dock and reconnecting a display. Fractional scaling can expose differences between native and XWayland applications. Text sharpness, pointer coordinates and capture resolution matter as much as whether a window opens.</p>
<p>For games, compare the same title, settings and runtime under controlled conditions if performance matters. Average FPS alone can hide frame-pacing or input problems. Overlays and recording may exercise another path entirely. No measurements were performed on Cthulhu for this article, so there is no invented latency or benchmark advantage to report.</p></section>

<section aria-labelledby="migration"><h2 id="migration">A migration plan with a real recovery path</h2>
<ol><li>List essential applications, shortcuts, devices and remote-access requirements.</li><li>Record the current distro, session, Plasma/KWin and driver versions.</li><li>Read the distribution’s upgrade notes and application-specific documentation.</li><li>Test on a supported session or suitable spare/live environment; hardware tests need real hardware.</li><li>Exercise the workflow checklist, including assistive technology and recovery access.</li><li>Upgrade through supported repositories and keep a usable backup and administration route.</li><li>Report a reproducible blocker with versions and conditions to the relevant project.</li></ol>
<p>Once 6.8 is installed, “select Plasma X11” is no longer a recovery plan for that release. A separate supported desktop, console access or a tested restore may be appropriate. Remaining temporarily on a supported older distribution is legitimate when essential work is blocked. Forcing scattered old Plasma packages into a new system is a different and much riskier proposition.</p>
<p>If you are planning a broader move to Linux, use this checklist alongside the <a href="/linux-migration-companion/">Linux Migration Companion</a> to inventory essential applications. Changing operating systems and changing session architecture are separate decisions; identifying indispensable workflows helps with both.</p>
<p>The useful conclusion is practical: X11 application compatibility survives, while assumptions about controlling the whole desktop deserve review. Plasma’s transition changes the session architecture. Whether it fits your machine is answered by the tasks you can complete reliably, not by a protocol argument on the Internet.</p></section>

